<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Harris, 1st Baron Harris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as General George Harris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Harris, 1st Baron Harris (1746–1829), was a British army officer who served in the American War of Independence, where he was wounded at Bunker Hill. As commander-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> army he led the British and Hyderabadi forces that invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore War</a> and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> in May 1799, ending the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>. He was made a baron in 1815.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Harris was born on 18 March 1746, the son of the Reverend George Harris, curate of Brasted in Kent. He was educated at Westminster School and the Royal Military Academy, Woolwich, was commissioned into the Royal Artillery in 1760 and transferred in 1762 as an ensign to the 5th Foot (Northumberland Fusiliers). He became lieutenant three years later and captain in 1771.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the American War of Independence he served at Lexington and Bunker Hill and in every engagement of Howe's army but one up to November 1778. He then served under Major-General William Medows at St. Lucia in 1778 and 1779, his regiment afterwards serving as marines in Rodney's fleet, and he was for a time a prisoner of war in 1779. Shortly before becoming lieutenant-colonel in 1780 he married Anne Carteret Dickson. After commanding the 5th in Ireland he went with Medows to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> until 1792, taking part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a>'s attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-mysore-war">Third Anglo-Mysore War</a>.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1794, he became major-general that year and in 1797 Commander-in-Chief of the Madras Army, for a short time also exercising the civil government of the presidency. In December 1798 Lord Mornington, the governor-general, gave him command of the field army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, and within a few months of campaigning he reduced the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a>, where Tipu died defending it. He received £100,000 in prize money and declined the offer of an Irish peerage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Harris returned home in 1800, became lieutenant-general in 1801 and full general in 1812, and bought Belmont House near Faversham. In 1815 he was created Baron Harris, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Seringapatam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and of Belmont in the County of Kent; he was made GCB in 1820 and governor of Dumbarton Castle in 1824, and was colonel of the 73rd (Highland) Regiment of Foot from 1800 until his death at Belmont on 19 May 1829. His son William succeeded him, and his descendant the 4th Baron Harris, a cricketer, was Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> from 1890 to 1895.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Harris, 1st Baron Harris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1799 British and Hyderabadi forces under <b>General George Harris</b> invaded Mysore in the Fourth Anglo-Mysore War, and on 4 May 1799 they stormed Srirangapatna.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brasted, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Kent, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,0.11&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Brasted on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George Harris, 1st Baron Harris"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5006 5743h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5006 5743h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,0.11&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
